<h2>How the Lead-to-Customer Conversion Calculator works</h2><p>Lead-to-customer conversion rate is customers acquired ÷ leads in the same defined population, multiplied by 100. The calculator also estimates how many leads would be required to produce a target number of customers if the current conversion rate remained unchanged.</p><h2>How to use this lead-to-customer conversion calculator</h2><p>Enter a lead cohort and the customers that came from that cohort whenever possible. For a short sales cycle, period-based counts may approximate cohorts; for a long cycle, leads created in one month may close in later months, so matching creation and conversion cohorts avoids false swings.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>The conversion percentage measures funnel efficiency, while the non-converted count translates the rate into volume. The “+1 percentage point” output shows the approximate additional customers from an absolute one-point conversion improvement at the current lead volume, which is different from a 1% relative improvement.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>Lead definitions vary widely: raw inquiries, MQLs, SQLs and accepted leads are different denominators. Duplicate leads, existing customers and disqualified records can distort the metric. The target-leads estimate assumes conversion stays constant as volume changes, which may fail if lead quality or channel mix changes.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>If 50 of 1,000 leads become customers, conversion is 5%. Reaching 80 customers at the same rate would require about 1,600 comparable leads. Alternatively, increasing conversion from 5% to 6% would add about 10 customers at the same 1,000-lead volume.</p>
